.TH CJSON_DICTIONARY_SET 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_dictionary_set \- set or replace a key in an object node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

ccol_retval_t cjson_dictionary_set(cjson obj, const char *key, cjson child);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets or replaces the value for key in obj.  If a previous child is
already under key, the library stores the new child first and
deep-frees the previous child only after the new child is safely in
place, so a failed insert never leaves the slot dangling.  On success
the object owns child and the caller must not free it.

The object keeps its members in insertion order: a new key goes after
every other member, and a replacement keeps the place of the key.  See
cjson_dictionary_first(3).

One return code carries one ownership rule, the same rule as for
cjson_list_push:

  ccol_invalid_args always means that the call rejected its arguments
  and touched nothing: child is exactly as it was before the call, and
  the caller still owns it, or the container that it is already
  attached to still owns it.  That owner must still destroy it.

  Every other non-success code (ccol_not_enough_memory,
  ccol_container_full) always means that ownership moved to this
  call, which has already deep-freed child, so a second free of child
  is a double free.

child must not already be attached to a list parent or to a
dictionary parent, with one exception: the caller can pass back the
exact node that is already stored under key, for example
cjson_dictionary_set(obj, k, cjson_dictionary_get(obj, k)).  This is a
harmless no-op that returns ccol_success and does not change obj.

These are the ccol_invalid_args rejections: child is NULL; child is
already attached somewhere else, under a different key or in another
container; child is obj itself; obj is NULL or is not a
CJSON_DICTIONARY; key is NULL or is not valid UTF-8.  There is one
more rejection: obj is already reachable when you descend from child.
Attaching child would then make obj a new ancestor of child while obj
stays a descendant of child, which is a cycle.

The library cannot always complete that last check when memory is
low.  Rather than take the risk of an undetected cycle, it then
returns ccol_not_enough_memory, and this call deep-frees child in the
same way as when the insert itself fails for lack of memory.

The expected time of a set, a get and a remove is constant, also when
an untrusted peer chooses the keys.  The object hashes its keys with a
secret seed that the library chooses one time for each process, and if
the keys collide, it changes to a hash keyed with
a secret 128-bit key; see cjson_parse(3).

Every key that the tree holds is valid UTF-8, which is what lets
cjson_serialize() write the key out unchanged.  The library refuses a
key that is not valid UTF-8 instead of storing it; see
cjson_create_string(3) for the same rule on string values.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
obj    Target dictionary node (must be CJSON_DICTIONARY).
key    Null-terminated key string, which must be valid UTF-8.  The
       library stores a copy of it.
child  Value node; must not already be attached elsewhere, and must
       not already contain obj in its own subtree (see above).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success, ccol_invalid_args, ccol_not_enough_memory, or
ccol_container_full if obj has already reached its maximum element
count.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A node that cjson_get(), cjson_list_get() or cjson_dictionary_get()
returns is a borrowed reference into a tree that already exists, not
a new node that the caller owns.  Never give such a node as child,
unless it is the value that is already stored under key (see above).
Use cjson_clone() first to get an independent copy, which is safe to
store somewhere else.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_dictionary_get (3),
.BR cjson_dictionary_first (3),
.BR cjson_create_dictionary (3),
.BR cjson_create_string (3),
.BR cjson_list_push (3),
.BR cjson_clone (3)
